     "text": "{\"completeness\": 0.9, \"citation_quality\": 0.85, \"severity_defensibility\": 0.88, \"overall_pass\": true, \"feedback\": \"This is a strong triage submission that correctly identifies the malicious activity and provides actionable recommendations. The analyst appropriately cited T1059.001, T1105, and T1071, though T1071 (Application Layer Protocol) is somewhat tangential since the focus is more on the download mechanism than protocol obfuscation\u2014T1086 (PowerShell) might have been more direct. The severity assessment is well-defended with clear reasoning about why this constitutes high-risk activity (confirmed execution, fileless malware delivery, internal endpoint). The response plan is comprehensive and prioritizes isolation and forensics appropriately. Minor improvements: explicitly note whether the MD5 hash 5d41402abc4b2a76b9719d911017c592 (which appears to be a test hash) matches any known malware signatures, and clarify the distinction between execution intent versus confirmed successful payload delivery\u2014while the PowerShell command shows clear intent, confirming whether the HTTP request succeeded would strengthen severity justification.\"}",
