   "container": null,
   "content": [
    {
     "citations": null,
     "text": "{\"attack_techniques\":[{\"id\":\"T1059.001\",\"name\":\"PowerShell\",\"tactics\":[\"Execution\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The alert shows PowerShell.exe being executed with specific flags (-NoProfile, -WindowStyle Hidden) to run the IEX command for remote code execution.\"},{\"id\":\"T1105\",\"name\":\"Ingress Tool Transfer\",\"tactics\":[\"Command and Control\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The command downloads a payload from an external malicious URL using Net.WebClient.DownloadString(), which is a common method for fetching tools or malware into the system.\"},{\"id\":\"T1071\",\"name\":\"Application Layer Protocol\",\"tactics\":[\"Command and Control\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The malware uses HTTP protocol to communicate with and download code from the remote malicious.com C2 server, blending the malicious traffic with normal web traffic.\"}],\"identity_signals\":null,\"key_findings\":\"PowerShell was executed on WORKSTATION-07 with obfuscation flags (-NoProfile, -WindowStyle Hidden) to download and execute code from a malicious external URL using IEX. The command line demonstrates classic fileless malware deployment via remote code execution. No identity signals were detected, but the endpoint activity itself constitutes confirmed malicious execution on an internal host.\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"severity_rationale\":\"This alert represents activity on an internal endpoint (WORKSTATION-07) where a user account executed a known malicious payload delivery pattern. The command demonstrates confirmed PowerShell-based RCE with intent to download additional malware, meeting the threshold of 'malware on an endpoint' and 'malicious execution on an internal host.' While there is no evidence of C2 communication completion or data exfiltration yet, the execution itself\u2014not blocked\u2014on an internal system elevates this to high severity.\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"recommended_response\":\"Immediately isolate WORKSTATION-07 from the network to prevent C2 callback and lateral movement. Collect full process memory and disk artifacts for forensics. Review endpoint logs for signs of payload execution and persistence mechanisms. Identify what was actually downloaded and executed from malicious.com. Assess whether domain\\\\jsmith's account has been compromised or if the host was already infected. Reset credentials for jsmith and review their recent access patterns. Scan all systems on the same network segment for similar indicators of compromise.\",\"rounds_used\":0}",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "diagnostics": null,
